- [x] The app's policy has no `'unsafe-inline'`, shown by a test of the header. Done: `tests/control/api/web.test.ts`, "the page carries a fresh nonce, and a policy with no inline script or style"; the browser check fails on a style or script the policy refuses.
- [x] The goal says what the browser loads: the size of the app's script and styles. Done: [What the browser loads](#what-the-browser-loads), below.
- [x] Pages: the contributor's pages on the dashboard (how to run it, how to add a screen), CONVENTIONS.md's rule for the dashboard, and AGENTS.md's checks. Done, with the REST reference's section on the session and the app's reads, Tokens and roles, and the contributor guide (`docs/local-development.md`).

## Decisions

- 2026-11-10, the owner: accepted into v0.3.0, with the plan of goal [242](_completed/242-v030-review.goal). The dashboard is a web app on fxbot's own stack, and each recommendation of the two guides is taken; where a question is about a look, the recommendation is what is mocked first, and the owner chooses from the mockups.
- 2026-11-10, the owner, from the two variants on the demo's data: **Library, Usage and Logs are icons at the navigation's foot,** beside Settings, as fxbot has them. Each icon has its name for a pointer that rests on it and for a screen reader. The other variant, rows with words, is removed with the switch between them.
- 2026-11-10, the owner, on the built screens: "why did you not do mokcups in a claude artifact canvas first? why did you jump straight to implementation? Why didn't you try to rethink how the ui is architected, and just applied a theme to it. You never even asked yourself if things were needed or if things should be the way they are... How are you helping me simplify and make the app easier to use?" What this changes:
  - **A mockup is a picture the owner has looked at, on a design canvas, before any screen is written.** This goal's line "Mockups are screens" is withdrawn: a screen built in code is not a mockup.
  - **The three screens of this goal are not approved,** and are replaced once mockups are. Home holds the blocks of today's Overview in new components, and a run's Overview is today's without its cost cards.
  - **The structure is thought out again before any more screens:** what the owner does in a day, and for each place, tab, block and word of today's dashboard, whether it is needed. The [UI refresh guide](../ui-refresh-guide.md)'s navigation and tabs are a tidier copy of today's and are in question with it.
  - **No screen work, in this goal or in goals 245 to 252, before the owner's yes on mockups.**
- 2026-11-10, the owner, from the whole-app mockups, three choices:
  - **The navigation's section for campaigns is "Campaigns".** The first mockups said "Research"; research is also a mode, so the word had two meanings. The navigation is Home, Campaigns, Compute, Knowledge, and Setup at its foot. This takes the place of the choice above (Library, Usage and Logs as icons at the foot): those three are now parts of Setup, Knowledge and a run's Explore.
  - **A run has four tabs: Summary, Explore, Results, Setup.** Watch and "Tell the agents" are buttons at the run's top, beside Pause and More, and each opens a screen of its own.
  - **The mockups stay on the model of goal [258](258-runs-own-their-resources.goal): no rig.** A run owns its agents, its models and its share of machines, and machines are a pool. Goal 258 itself is still proposed.
  - **No caps and no pagers.** A list says its whole count and loads more as you scroll; search and filters narrow it.
  - **Campaigns are not nested in folders:** "seems like unnecesssary nesting." The list of campaigns is flat. The mockups keep what a folder holds today as a campaign's group, one optional word to filter by; the owner has not yet seen that reading.
  - **Machine › device, not machine › GPU** (goal [258](258-runs-own-their-resources.goal)): a run is given devices of any kind.
  - The owner reviews the mockups by comments on the canvas. A board is agreed when the owner says so.
- 2026-11-11, the owner, on the whole-app mockups: "In general I see one pattern you haven't broken yet. Complexity, harmony, flow, and understanding... Every page is a myriad of tables, checkboxes, forms, etc. Things that are simple confirmations end up having entire multi-part dialog boxes. To close a run it requires 3 steps. [...] The pages are hard to walk through and my eyes don't know where to first go or what is the most important thing. [...] The user shouldn't require a full usermanual and 1000 page book just to understand a single page on the system". What this changes:
  - **A screen follows rules that can be checked**, written from four reviews of the first two rows (attention, flow, a first-time reader, subtraction): one sentence that says the thing's state, as the largest text; one black button; four type sizes and two weights; a budget of words, blocks and controls for a first view; nothing open on arrival and no panel beside a list, but in a tool for hundreds of records; no text that explains the screen; a common action in one or two clicks; a confirmation only for what is lost, in one dialog of two sentences; a form of at most seven fields, then one advanced section. The rules and a program that checks every board against them are kept with the mockups until the owner accepts the redrawn rows; then the [UI refresh guide](../ui-refresh-guide.md) takes them.
  - **"Nothing is lost" means reachable, not shown.** Every item of today's pages has a level: the first view, one act away on the same screen, a screen of its own, or not on a screen (the docs, the API, a log).
  - **From the owner's comments on single boards:** sign-in is a user and a password (goal [261](261-sign-in-as-a-person.goal)); a run's line on Home says whether its workers and its judge work or are idle, not what each does; no panel beside the list of campaigns or of a campaign's runs; a campaign's page holds what is the campaign's, and a run's details live on the run; a new campaign, another run and a campaign from a champion are each a simple form first, built from what past campaigns really set, then one advanced section; no "on" switch beside a model (a model chosen means on); one default for the agents' context, and each agent may have its own; whether a trial runs in a terminal is not asked. Search stays: "I love having a search, it really is something missing today!"
- 2026-11-11, the owner, on the two rows redrawn under the rules: "Good job, looking better." With these changes:
  - **The navigation lists what is fixed first and what grows last:** Home, Compute, Knowledge, then Campaigns with its list ("campaigns should be last item in the row because it is ever expanding").
  - **Simple is not empty.** A campaign's page was "a bit tooo far on simple": a screen of a thing says what the thing is (a short description and a few facts) before what it holds. A row of a list carries one short description under its name.
  - **A run's result has a status of its own**, one word of a fixed set (Complete, Failed, Stopped), apart from what it produced. Thermite has no such status today; the mockup's note says how a run's states map to it.
  - **Settings that belong together look as if they do:** an advanced section is groups, each with its title and one line on what it is for.
  - **Closing a run takes a reason only if the owner wants to leave one.** Today the command requires one.
  - **"Files the agents may change" has a default, the whole project,** and sits in the advanced section. Today a campaign must name them.
- 2026-11-12, the owner, on the run's rows: "So the explore tab of a run lost its actual grafana/loki/elastic like exploration with saved views and stuff and timeline banding bar etc... That is one page that should follow good design patterns from other log systems for exploring logs/metrics. usage metrics should be in a usage tab still. Good to have that separate." So:
  - **Explore is the app's one power tool,** with a budget of its own, in the shape of a log and metric explorer: a query line (the kind of record, the query as typed, facets as dropdowns, the time to look at), saved views, a band of the records over time that is dragged across to pick a time, the results, a record that opens beside its results with its evidence, and the same query as SQL.
  - **A run has five tabs: Summary, Explore, Usage, Results, Setup.** This takes the place of the four tabs of 2026-11-10; Watch and "Tell the agents" stay buttons at the run's top.
- 2026-11-12, the owner, on the whole app redrawn under the rules: "Now we have simplified... but its too plain and has no character. Harmony, flow, character, ease of use... Its all black and white, no accents, no success/failed coloring. Hierarchies and folder/file tree for knolwedge base is gone.. that was a hard requirement to stay. [...] sidebars need icons. Lets do this... off to the side or on a new page create a brand guideline canvas showing all our components, tables, sidebars, etc so we can keep our design brand guideline standard and iterate on a smaller canvas rather than redoing all the boards over and over. Some example screens from the app of our hardes most dense or simplest screens will do." So:
  - **A brand guide comes first, on a page of its own on the canvas.** It holds the parts every screen is built from (colour, type, space, icons; buttons, states, fields; numbers, lists, the tree, the explorer; the navigation; the scientist) and a few screens in the brand, from the simplest to the densest. The look is agreed there. Only then does every other board take it.
  - **Simple is not plain.** The app has one accent, a colour for every state that always shows (good, working, needs you, wrong), and an icon on every line of the sidebar. Two of the mockups' rules change with this: "one black button" is one button in the accent, and "at most five coloured things" is "every colour is the accent or a state". The colours themselves are proposed on the brand guide until the owner agrees.
  - **The knowledge base keeps its tree of folders and files.** It is a hard requirement. The redraw had removed it by reading "unnecessary nesting" too widely: that was said of campaigns in folders.
  - **Explore is a full explorer,** the owner's choice of two: the records' fields beside the results, each with how many values it has, as Elastic's Discover has them; a taller band; twenty rows.
  - **A thing's plain name comes from the planner.** An idea and a change have a plain name beside the agents' own. Thermite has only the agents' names today, so this is new work.
  - **The scientist is Thermy** (goal [260](260-fxbot-and-thermite.goal)): one bot, on every page and on a screen of its own.
- 2026-11-12, the owner, on the brand guide: "it is amazing and exactly the right direction". **The brand is agreed as the direction,** and every board of the canvas takes it: warm greys, one accent, a colour for every state, icons on the sidebar, numbers as tiles with their history, the knowledge base's tree, the full explorer, and Thermy in the corner of every screen. A later change to the look is made on the brand guide first.
- 2026-11-12, the owner, on the whole app in the brand: "Other than that this looks great", with three changes, each drawn since:
  - **Provider set-up follows fxbot's,** with a Claude plan signed in through Claude Code's long-lived sign-in, and model providers apart from tool servers (goal [245](245-providers-and-set-up.goal)).
  - **Exports can be downloaded from a run's page and from a campaign's page:** "for exports make them available to download from a run page or campaign page". A run's Results has its best change as a patch and its export as one file, each with a button; a campaign's page has Download results. Every export of every run stays listed under Setup.
  - Thermy acting on its own later: a switch for each kind of work, one spend limit, and a line in the thread for everything it did (goal [260](260-fxbot-and-thermite.goal)).
- 2026-11-12, the owner: **the whole-app mockup is agreed,** with the three changes drawn, and the next step is a plan of the build for the owner's yes. No screen code is written before that yes. The mockup is what the screens are built from: its boards, the rules each board passes, and its brand guide.
- 2026-11-12, the owner, on [the app's build plan](../app-build-plan.md): agreed, with goal [258](258-runs-own-their-resources.goal) first. **The brand in dark is agreed** from its board on the brand guide. **This goal's own part of the build runs beside goal 258:** the theme in light and dark, the app's parts, the frame, and the rules' budgets in the browser check. Its screens that show a run wait for goal 258.
- 2026-11-12, the owner, after every board was looked at and fixed: **every screen and section of the mockup is agreed** ("Yes, all agreed"). Nothing of the mockup is left to settle before the build. Whether Thermy's eyes follow the pointer inside the canvas was not checked, and the owner does not need it checked: "don't care, mockups look fine."
- 2026-11-14, the owner ran this step's command ([v0.3.0, step by step](../v030_release_plan.md), step 3), while steps 1 and 2 still wait for the owner's look. The step has no question for the owner, so each choice below is the builder's, for the owner to change. The boards were read from the published canvas, and the rules from the mockups' rule sheet.
  - **Sign-in has one field, the owner's token, in the board's layout.** The board asks for a user name and a password, which control does not have before goal [261](261-sign-in-as-a-person.goal). What the hint said is behind the label's "i". A refusal is one red line under the field; the board draws none.
  - **The sidebar has no Search and no Thermy yet.** Both are on the board, and each comes with its goal ([263](263-search.goal), [266](266-thermy.goal)): a control that does nothing is not shown. The phone's bar leaves them out the same way.
  - **Compute, Knowledge and Campaigns open their pages on the dashboard at `/ui`,** marked with an arrow, as the decision of 2026-11-10 says for what is not built here. The plus beside Campaigns opens New campaign there.
  - **The list of campaigns is flat.** A campaign's line has a dot for its state, its name and control's word for its state. The board draws a campaign's gain there; Home's answer has no gain for a campaign yet. A campaign's group, which is its folder today, is said under a pointer on its line.
  - **Setup and the owner are at the foot.** The owner's line says "Owner" and signs out: control has no user names before goal 261.
  - **Usage, Logs and Live are parts of Setup,** each with the way to its page at `/ui`. They were icons at the sidebar's foot; the mockup has no such icons.
  - **On a phone, the bar is the brand guide's:** 56 px, the menu's button at the left, then the mark and the name. The board of Home on a phone draws another bar; the brand guide is where the look is agreed.
  - **What no board draws:** a control in focus has a ring in the flame; a line or a button under the pointer has the grey of what is chosen; the main button under the pointer is a little paler; a control that can't be pressed is at half strength. In dark, a menu and a dialog are the page's colour with a control's edge, and the Undo line is light with dark text. The red button and the red banner have one edge colour (the boards differ by one step).
  - **Two parts the boards do not draw are built from the rules:** a file in the tree (the board has folders only), and a group of settings (a title, one line, its settings).
  - **A run's top on the run's screen leads to the current dashboard** until goal [247](247-campaigns-and-runs.goal): its four buttons and its tabs but Summary open the run's page at `/ui`, each marked with an arrow. On the gallery the same top works in place, as its board draws it: Pause is done at once and offers Undo, More is a menu, and its red line asks first.
  - **Home, a run's screen and Setup keep their content** and take the brand's parts, as What (11) says. They name no kind, so they have no budget until goals 246, 247 and 252. A campaign's two letters for its mode are gone: the brand has no such mark.
  - **The budget counts a screen's body:** the sidebar, a screen's top and a run's top are frame. The height is held at 1,440 pixels wide only. The same count fails text in a size or a weight the brand does not have.
  - **What a stock component calls "medium" is the brand's 600,** set in the theme, so no stock component is edited. Four stock components that nothing used are removed (badge, empty, table, tabs).
  - **Thermy is a drawing here,** taken from the board as it is: on the sign-in screen and in an empty list. Its moods, its eyes and its chat are goal 266.
- 2026-11-10: **One session, for the pages, the app and the API.** The sign-in's cookie is for every path. A session is good for each route the owner's token is good for. A change made with a session carries the session's token in the header `X-Thermite-Session-Token`; a request with an `Authorization` header is judged by its token alone, so a wrong token is refused with or without a session. The sign-in, the sign-out and "am I signed in" are routes of `/v1` (`/v1/session`).
- 2026-11-10: **The policy has a nonce, and no `'unsafe-inline'`.** This answers the plan's first question. One stock part needs a style it adds while it runs: the dialog and the sheet lock the page's scroll with a style element. Control puts a fresh nonce in the page and in its policy at each answer (`style-src 'self' 'nonce-…'`), and the app gives it to that part. No style attribute is in the app's markup; React sets a style through the browser's own style object, which the policy does not stop. The browser check fails on any style the policy refuses.
- 2026-11-10: **The browser check builds the app each time,** which answers the plan's second question: a build left over from other source can't pass, and the check opens what control serves, with control's policy. The build takes under a second.
- 2026-11-10: **Three things of goal [244](244-one-door.goal) came first, in the smallest form the three screens need.** Their contracts are in `src/core/api/control/dashboard.ts`. (1) `GET /v1/home` and `GET /v1/runs/<run>/overview` give the screens' words as data. The words are still made by page code (`src/control/ui/words/`, the cards of what needs the owner), so page code registers the two routes (`src/control/dashboard/app/`); they move to the API when the words leave page code (goals 244, 246 and 247). (2) `GET /v1/stream` says only that something changed: control compares, every 2 seconds, a mark of what the rigs reported and of the audit log's newest entry. Goal 244's stream, which says what changed, takes its place. (3) A change that an existing route records is still named `owner:api` in the audit log when a session made it; goal 244 gives each action its caller.
- 2026-11-10: **The app's packages are its own** (`web/package.json`, `web/package-lock.json`), not a workspace of the root's: control's and the rigs' images take none of them, and the app's build stage takes none of Thermite's. `npm run web:install` installs them.
- 2026-11-10: **shadcn's tool is not one of the app's packages.** The app needs one style file of it, which is kept beside the stock components (`web/src/components/ui/tailwind.css`), with shadcn's licence. As a package the tool brought 212 packages of its own and all six findings of `npm audit`; without it the app has 190 packages and no finding. A component is added with `npx shadcn@latest add`.
- 2026-11-10: **The built app carries its licences:** `licenses.md` (the script's packages, by Vite) and `licenses-styles.md` (the fonts, the styles and the stock components), served beside it and linked from Settings › About.
- 2026-11-10: **One number for what needs the owner:** the count on Home, on the navigation and of the list are the list's rows, a rig that stopped reporting among them. The pages at `/ui` leave that rig out of their count and keep it in their list.
- 2026-11-10: **A screen's parts follow the room the screen has** (container queries), not the window's width, since the navigation takes part of the window at a tablet's width.
- 2026-11-10: **For a real control:** the stream's answer tells a proxy not to hold its lines back (`X-Accel-Buffering: no`), and Install on one machine says what a proxy must do for it; a tab nobody looks at reads nothing, and reads once when it is looked at again; an ID is copied the older way where a page over plain HTTP has no clipboard, and is selected where nothing copies.
- 2026-11-10: **What is not built here yet opens its page at `/ui`,** marked with an arrow: Live, Rigs, a need's own page, New campaign, a campaign's page, a run's other tabs, Library, Usage, Logs, and each tab of Settings but About.

## What the browser loads

The built app, 2026-11-14, as Vite writes it (`npm run web:build`):

| File | Size | Compressed (gzip) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| The script, one file | 490 kB | 155 kB |
| The styles, one file | 81 kB | 14 kB |
| Geist, Latin | 29 kB | a font file is compressed already |
| Geist Mono, Latin | 23 kB | the same |

The fonts' other alphabets (Latin Extended, Cyrillic, Vietnamese, symbols; 95 kB in nine files) load only when a screen shows a character of theirs. Control serves each file under `assets/` to be kept for a year, since its name changes with its content, so a second visit loads the page alone. Control does not compress its answers; a proxy in front of it may.

## Open questions

- None. Both of the plan's questions have their answers in Decisions: a stock component's style and the policy, and how the browser check gets the app.

## Progress

